  "answer": "Current web sources (retrieved October 27, 2026) show that at the summit of Mount Everest (elevation ~8,848–8,849 m / 29,029–29,032 ft), atmospheric pressure drops to approximately 34 kPa (about 255 Torr, or roughly one-third of sea-level pressure) [W3], [W5], [W6].\n\nBecause of this substantially lower pressure, water boils at approximately **68 °C to 72 °C (154 °F to 161.5 °F)**, depending on the atmospheric model and barometric conditions [W1], [W2], [W3], [W4], [W5], [W6], [W7]:\n\n- A widely cited standard-atmosphere value is **71 °C (160 °F)** at ~34 kPa [W6].\n- Elevation and vapor-pressure calculations typically place it between **70 °C and 70.3 °C (158.5 °F)** [W5], [W7] and approximately **72 °C (161.5 °F)** [W2], [W3].\n- Other educational sources report it as approximately **68 °C (154 °F)** [W1], [W4]."
